WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.617
SQL is written in one order, but it is useful to think about its logical processing in another

00:00:06.617 --> 00:00:14.827
order. For a simple query, PostgreSQL first identifies the source in FROM, then applies WHERE to filter rows.

00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:18.192
SELECT defines the result expressions.

00:00:18.192 --> 00:00:21.307
DISTINCT can remove repeated result rows.

00:00:21.307 --> 00:00:24.707
ORDER BY sorts the remaining result, and LIMIT keeps the requested number of rows.

00:00:24.707 --> 00:00:32.247
This mental model helps explain why filtering happens before sorting.

00:00:32.247 --> 00:00:39.592
It also helps explain why an alias created in SELECT is not always available in earlier logical stages.

00:00:39.592 --> 00:00:42.102
You do not need to memorize database-engine internals.

00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:43.947
Use this as a reasoning model.

00:00:43.947 --> 00:00:52.022
When a query behaves differently from what you expected, ask which stage creates or removes the value you

00:00:52.022 --> 00:00:59.847
are trying to use. Connect this idea to an analyst exploring operational records before building a report.

00:00:59.847 --> 00:01:05.512
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:05.512 --> 00:01:13.187
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:13.187 --> 00:01:18.717
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:24.401
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
